"Connects time-ordered measurements to show the direction and rate of change in a quantitative series.\n\nUse when: Observations measure a comparable quantity over ordered time. Readers need trends, timing, or changes between successive measurements.\n\nAvoid when: Categories are unordered or connecting observations would imply nonexistent continuity.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes elapsed time with proportional spacing. Vertical position encodes the measured value; a separate path identifies each series.\n\nBuild: Sort timestamps and resolve duplicate-time aggregation explicitly. Draw lines through valid measurements and break paths at meaningful missing intervals. Label units and series near clear endpoints outside the data paths.\n\nCheck: Check time spacing is proportional rather than categorical. Verify any narrowed vertical range is labeled and justified by the comparison. Confirm missing measurements are not silently converted to zero.\n\nPitfalls: Line crossings encode equal values or ordering changes; never move values to untangle them. Prefer separate panels when crossings prevent series tracking, and avoid smoothing that invents extrema.\n\nExample: Plot synthetic daily water consumption for one month, retaining a visible gap where the meter was offline.\n\nAlternatives: Step chart, Indexed line chart, Confidence band."
